        text: '(1) When any person has reason to believe that he may be arrested on an accusation of having committed a non‑bailable offence, he may apply to the High Court or the Court of Session for a direction under this section; and that Court may, if it thinks fit, direct that in the event of such arrest, he shall be released on bail. (2) When the High Court or the Court of Session makes a direction under sub-section (1), it may include such conditions in such directions in the light of the facts of the particular case, as it may think fit, including— (i) a condition that the person shall make himself available for interrogation by a police officer as and when required; (ii) a condition that the person shall not, directly or indirectly, make any inducement, threat or promise to any person acquainted with the facts of the case so as to dissuade him from disclosing such facts to the Court or to any police officer; (iii) a condition that the person shall not leave India without the previous permission of the Court; (iv) such other condition as may be imposed under sub-section (3) of section 480, as if the bail were granted under that section. (3) If such person is thereafter arrested without warrant by an officer in charge of a police station on such accusation, and is prepared either at the time of arrest or at any time while in the custody of such officer to give bail, he shall be released on bail; and if a Magistrate taking cognizance of such offence decides that a warrant should be issued in the first instance against that person, he shall issue a bailable warrant in conformity with the direction of the Court under sub-section (1). (4) Nothing in this section shall apply to any case involving the arrest of any person on accusation of having committed an offence under section 65 and sub-section (2) of section 70 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. [Note: this is "anticipatory" bail — for a person who has not yet been arrested but has reason to believe they may be — and can only be applied for before the High Court or Court of Session, never a Magistrate. It corresponds to Section 438 of the repealed CrPC, 1973; unlike old Section 438(1), this section\'s main clause does not itself list the factors (nature/gravity of accusation, antecedents, flight risk, etc.) the court must weigh — commentators have flagged this as a widening of judicial discretion, though the substance is still applied via case law. Sub-section (4) bars anticipatory bail for the aggravated sexual-offence provisions at Sections 65 and 70(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.]',
