        heading: 'Bar to taking cognizance after lapse of the period of limitation',
        text: '468. Bar to taking cognizance after lapse of the period of limitation.—(1) Except as otherwise provided elsewhere in this Code, no Court shall take cognizance of an offence of the category specified in sub-section (2), after the expiry of the period of limitation. (2) The period of limitation shall be— (a) six months, if the offence is punishable with fine only; (b) one year, if the offence is punishable with imprisonment for a term not exceeding one year; (c) three years, if the offence is punishable with imprisonment for a term exceeding one year but not exceeding three years. (3) For the purposes of this section, the period of limitation, in relation to offences which may be tried together, shall be determined with reference to the offence which is punishable with the more severe punishment or, as the case may be, the most severe punishment. [Note: this section has been repealed and replaced by Section 514 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (also in this Library), with effect from 1 July 2024. BNSS Section 514 adds an Explanation fixing the "relevant date" for computing limitation as the date of filing a complaint under BNSS section 223 or the date of recording information under BNSS section 173, which has no equivalent here.]',
